+  <h1>30-Day Step Challenge: A Week-by-Week Plan You’ll Actually Finish</h1>
+  <p class="blog-meta">September 27, 2026 · 7 min read</p>
+
+  <p>Most 30-day step challenges start at 10,000 steps on day one. That’s fine if you already walk
+    that much. If you don’t, it means sore feet by day four, a missed day by day six and a quiet exit by
+    day ten. A challenge you finish beats one that sounds impressive, so this plan starts from
+    <strong>your</strong> current number and builds up week by week.</p>
+  <p>Below is the full 30-day schedule, ways to hit your target on busy days, and how to run the
+    challenge with friends so it lasts past week two.</p>
+
+  <h2>Step 1: Find your baseline (days 1–3)</h2>
+  <p>For the first three days, walk as you normally would and note your daily step count from your
+    phone or watch. Add the three days together and divide by three. That’s your
+    <strong>baseline</strong>. Round it to the nearest 500.</p>
+  <p>Don’t try to impress yourself here. An honest baseline is what makes the rest of the plan
+    doable. If your baseline is 4,000, that’s a perfectly good place to start.</p>
+
+  <h2>Step 2: Follow the four-week ramp</h2>
+  <p>Each week raises your daily target a little. The jumps are small enough that no single day feels
+    like a big effort, but by the end you’re walking thousands more steps a day than when you
+    started.</p>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>Days 4–10 (week 1):</strong> baseline + 500 steps a day</li>
+    <li><strong>Days 11–17 (week 2):</strong> baseline + 1,500 steps a day</li>
+    <li><strong>Days 18–24 (week 3):</strong> baseline + 2,500 steps a day</li>
+    <li><strong>Days 25–30 (week 4):</strong> baseline + 3,500 steps a day</li>
+  </ul>
+  <p><strong>Example:</strong> with a 5,000-step baseline, your targets are 5,500 → 6,500 → 7,500 →
+    8,500. With a 7,000 baseline, you finish the month at 10,500 a day, and you got there gradually.</p>
+  <p>If a week feels too hard, repeat it instead of moving up. The plan is there to help you, not to
+    test you.</p>
+
+  <h2>Step 3: Build in one flex day a week</h2>
+  <p>Life happens: travel days, sick days, a 14-hour workday. Give yourself one
+    <strong>flex day per week</strong> where the target is just your baseline. It stops one bad day from
+    feeling like you’ve failed the whole challenge, which is the moment most people quit. (It’s the
+    same idea as a <a href="/blog/streak-freezes-explained">streak freeze</a>.)</p>
+
+  <h2>12 ways to add steps on a busy day</h2>
+  <p>Roughly speaking, 1,000 steps is about a 10-minute walk at an easy pace, though it varies with
+    your stride. A few short walks add up fast:</p>
+  <ol>
+    <li>Take phone calls standing up and walking</li>
+    <li>Do a 10-minute walk after each main meal</li>
+    <li>Get off the bus or train one stop early</li>
+    <li>Park at the far end of the car park</li>
+    <li>Use the stairs for anything under four floors</li>
+    <li>Turn one weekly coffee meeting into a walking meeting</li>
+    <li>Walk laps while you wait (for the kettle, a pickup, a download)</li>
+    <li>Walk to a colleague’s desk instead of messaging</li>
+    <li>Take the long route to the bathroom or kitchen</li>
+    <li>Walk while listening to a podcast episode you’d play anyway</li>
+    <li>Do an evening loop around the block before dinner</li>
+    <li>Walk the dog one extra time, or offer to walk a neighbour’s</li>
+  </ol>
+
+  <h2>Run it with friends (this is what gets you to day 30)</h2>
+  <p>Solo challenges depend on willpower. Group challenges depend on people noticing, and that
+    lasts longer. But group challenges have a well-known failure pattern: a few people race ahead, the
+    rest stop checking, and it’s quiet by week two. We break this down in
+    <a href="/blog/why-group-fitness-challenges-fail">why most group fitness challenges fail by day 3</a>.
+    Three fixes make the difference:</p>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>Count steps automatically.</strong> Manual check-ins and screenshots are the first thing
+      people stop doing. Use an app that reads steps straight from Apple Health or Health Connect.</li>
+    <li><strong>Make the goal reachable for everyone.</strong> Personal targets (like the ramp above) or
+      a team total mean the least active person can still contribute.</li>
+    <li><strong>Keep a live leaderboard.</strong> Seeing a friend 300 steps ahead at 9pm has sent many
+      people out for one more walk.</li>
+  </ul>
+  <p>For a quick boost mid-challenge, add a 1-on-1 race: pick the friend just ahead of you and race
+    them to a step count by the end of the day. In Sweats that’s one tap: <em>Beat this</em> on their
+    post starts a <a href="/fitness-duels">duel</a>.</p>
+
+  <h2>What to do on day 31</h2>
+  <p>Finishing is the goal, but the real prize is a higher normal. On day 31, set your new baseline to
+    your week-three target, not the peak. That’s a level you can realistically keep every day. Then
+    pick your next challenge: a distance goal, active minutes, or a
+    <a href="/blog/workplace-fitness-challenge-ideas">team challenge at work</a>.</p>
+
+  <h2>A quick safety note</h2>
+  <p>Walking is one of the easiest activities to add, but increase gradually, wear comfortable shoes,
+    and check with a doctor before starting if you have a health condition, an injury or you’ve
+    been mostly inactive for a long time.</p>
+
