+<article class="legal-page">
+  <a href="/blog/" class="back-link">← Back to the blog</a>
+  <span class="blog-tag">Consistency</span>
+  <h1>How to Stay Consistent With Working Out: 9 Habits That Actually Stick</h1>
+  <p class="blog-meta">September 27, 2026 · 8 min read</p>
+
+  <p>Almost everyone can start working out. The hard part is week three, when the new-routine buzz
+    wears off, work gets busy and skipping one session quietly turns into skipping ten. Consistency
+    isn’t a personality trait some people have and others don’t. It mostly comes from setting
+    things up so that showing up is easier than skipping.</p>
+  <p>Here are nine habits that make that happen. None of them needs more willpower, and most take less
+    than five minutes to set up.</p>
+
+  <h2>1. Set a minimum you can hit on your worst day</h2>
+  <p>Most routines fail because the plan was built for your best week. “Five one-hour gym sessions”
+    works until you get a cold, a deadline or a trip. Keep your ideal plan, but add a
+    <strong>floor</strong>: the smallest session that still counts. A 10-minute walk. One set of
+    push-ups. Fifteen minutes on the bike.</p>
+  <p>On a bad day you do the floor and the day still counts. You’ll often do more once you’ve
+    started. The main job of the floor is to stop “I can’t do the full workout” from becoming
+    “I did nothing.”</p>
+
+  <h2>2. Plan for missed days in advance</h2>
+  <p>You will miss days. Deciding ahead of time what a miss means is what stops one bad day from
+    ending the whole routine. A simple rule works well: <strong>never miss twice in a row.</strong>
+    One missed day is life. Two in a row starts a new habit you didn’t want.</p>
+  <p>This is also why all-or-nothing streaks can backfire. If one missed day resets you to zero, the
+    “logical” move after missing is to stop caring. A streak that allows a limited number of freezes
+    teaches the opposite: a miss is recoverable, so come back tomorrow. We go deeper on this in
+    <a href="/blog/streak-freezes-explained">streak freezes explained</a>.</p>
+
+  <h2>3. Tie your workout to a fixed time or trigger</h2>
+  <p>“I’ll work out sometime today” loses to everything else that happens today. Attach the session to
+    something that already happens every day: after the school drop-off, straight from work before you
+    get home, or before your first coffee on weekends. Putting it on a fixed slot in your calendar means you
+    decide once instead of every day.</p>
+
+  <h2>4. Make it social</h2>
+  <p>This is the most reliable habit on the list. When someone else expects you to show up, or will
+    notice if you don’t, skipping gets much harder. That’s why a lot of people who struggle alone do
+    fine in a run club, a class or with a training partner.</p>
+  <p>You don’t need a formal group. One of these is enough:</p>
+  <ul>
+    <li>A friend you text before and after each session</li>
+    <li>A weekly session you do with someone at a fixed time</li>
+    <li>A small group where everyone’s workouts are visible to each other</li>
+  </ul>
+  <p>We cover why this works so well in <a href="/blog/why-training-alone-doesnt-work">why training
+    alone doesn’t work</a>, and how to set it up in
+    <a href="/blog/workout-accountability-partner">how to find a workout accountability partner</a>.</p>
+
+  <h2>5. Add a little friendly competition</h2>
+  <p>Some people respond to a goal. Many more respond to a person. A friendly bet or a short
+    head-to-head challenge (“most steps by Sunday”) gives your workouts a reason and a deadline. Keep
+    the stakes small and the rounds short, so losing one is funny rather than discouraging and you can
+    start again straight away.</p>
+  <p>In Sweats you can turn any friend’s workout into a private 1-on-1 race with one tap. See
+    <a href="/fitness-duels">how fitness duels work</a>.</p>
+
+  <h2>6. Track progress you can actually see</h2>
+  <p>Motivation drops when effort feels invisible. Keep a record that shows progress at a glance: a
+    calendar of training days, a weekly total, a route map, a monthly distance. The format matters less
+    than two things: logging has to take no effort (ideally automatic from your phone or watch), and you
+    have to look at it regularly.</p>
+  <p>A short weekly recap works well. Every Sunday, look at how many days you trained and one number
+    that improved. A few seconds of that is surprisingly motivating.</p>
+
+  <h2>7. Remove friction the night before</h2>
+  <p>Many skipped workouts are decided the evening before without you noticing. The kit isn’t packed,
+    the plan is vague, the alarm isn’t set. Cut the number of decisions standing between you and the
+    workout:</p>
+  <ul>
+    <li>Put your clothes out (or pack the gym bag) the night before</li>
+    <li>Decide tomorrow’s session tonight: what, where and for how long</li>
+    <li>Keep a “default workout” for days when you can’t decide</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <h2>8. Choose a workout you don’t hate</h2>
+  <p>The best training plan is one you’ll still be doing in six months. If you dread running, a
+    running plan will lose to your sofa every time. Try a few types of training (cycling, swimming,
+    strength, yoga, hiking, classes) and build around whatever you look forward to, or at least don’t
+    mind. You can add variety later. Just getting to the workout comes first.</p>
+
+  <h2>9. Get a nudge at the right moment</h2>
+  <p>Generic daily reminders get ignored within a week. A nudge that’s specific and well timed
+    works much better: <em>“Your streak ends at midnight; a 15-minute walk keeps it.”</em> That can be a
+    friend who checks in, a calendar alert with a specific plan, or an app that reads your training and
+    only speaks up when it matters.</p>
+  <p>The Sweats AI coach is built for that last one. It messages you first when your streak is at
+    risk, when you’ve had a strong week, or when you’re about to lose a duel.
+    <a href="/ai-fitness-coach">See how it works</a>.</p>
+
+  <h2>Putting it together: a one-week starter plan</h2>
+  <ol>
+    <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Write down your ideal week <em>and</em> your floor workout.</li>
+    <li><strong>Day 2:</strong> Put three fixed training slots in your calendar for the week.</li>
+    <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Ask one friend to train with you, or check in with you, this week.</li>
+    <li><strong>Day 4:</strong> Set up automatic tracking so logging takes no effort.</li>
+    <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Start a small challenge with that friend, such as most active minutes by Sunday.</li>
+    <li><strong>Day 6–7:</strong> Do a quick weekly review: days trained, one number that improved, next week’s slots.</li>
+  </ol>
+  <p>You don’t need a perfect week. You need one that’s slightly easier to repeat than the last, and
+    to keep repeating it.</p>
+
+  <div class="blog-cta">
