+  <h1>Fitness Duels: 1-on-1 Workout Challenges With Friends</h1>
+  <p>A friend just posted an 8.4 km run. You think you could beat it. In Sweats, that thought takes
+    two taps to turn into a real race: hit <strong>Beat this</strong> on their post, pick how long you
+    have, and it becomes a private 1-on-1 duel. The app picks a fair metric, counts your workouts
+    automatically, and tells you the moment the lead changes.</p>
+  <p>Duels are free and unlimited. You don’t need a group, a spreadsheet or a plan — just one
+    friend and one number to beat.</p>
+
+  <div class="blog-cta">
+    <p>Challenge a friend to your first duel today.</p>
+    <a href="/#download" class="btn btn-primary">Get Sweats free</a>
+  </div>
+
+  <div class="post-image">
+    <picture><source srcset="./assets/screens/challenge.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="./assets/screens/challenge.jpg" alt="Sweats challenge screen showing a live head-to-head leaderboard between friends" loading="lazy" /></picture>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2>How a fitness duel works</h2>
+  <ol>
+    <li><strong>Find a workout to beat.</strong> Every activity post in your feed has a
+      <em>Beat this</em> button: runs, rides, walks, swims or gym sessions.</li>
+    <li><strong>Pick your time window.</strong> A few hours if you can go right now, or until
+      tomorrow if you can’t.</li>
+    <li><strong>Sweats picks a fair metric.</strong> Distance, steps, workout minutes or calories,
+      depending on what the original workout measured best. You can’t change the terms once the duel
+      starts.</li>
+    <li><strong>Train as usual.</strong> Workouts you record in Sweats, plus steps and activity from
+      Apple Health on iPhone, count on their own. There’s nothing to log by hand.</li>
+    <li><strong>First to the number wins.</strong> If neither of you gets there, whoever is ahead when
+      time runs out wins. The winner gets a share card.</li>
+  </ol>
+
+  <h2>Why a 1-on-1 challenge works better than a group one</h2>
+  <p>Big group challenges tend to lose steam. A few people pull ahead, everyone else stops checking
+    the leaderboard, and by the second week it’s quiet. We wrote about that pattern in
+    <a href="/blog/why-group-fitness-challenges-fail">why most group fitness challenges fail by day 3</a>.</p>
+  <p>A duel avoids that pattern for three reasons:</p>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>You’re racing a person, not a goal.</strong> It’s easy to let yourself off a
+      goal. It’s much harder to lose to a friend who will see the result.</li>
+    <li><strong>The stakes are small and they reset.</strong> Losing a duel costs you one round, not
+      a 40-day streak. You can start another one straight away, which is why people keep playing.
+      (More on that in <a href="/blog/the-case-for-fitness-duels">the case for duels</a>.)</li>
+    <li><strong>It’s private.</strong> Only the two of you can see a duel, so there’s no public
+      record of losses to put people off trying.</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <h2>What counts toward a duel</h2>
+  <p>Every duel is measured on one of four metrics: <strong>distance</strong>, <strong>steps</strong>,
+    <strong>workout minutes</strong> or <strong>calories</strong>. Sweats picks the one that makes sense
+    for the workout you’re racing, so a runner and a lifter are never scored on a metric that only suits one of them.</p>
+  <p>GPS workouts you record in Sweats count directly, and Sweats also reads steps and workouts from
+    Apple Health on iPhone. The number comes from the tracking, not from anyone typing it in, so
+    nobody has to trust anyone else’s maths.</p>
+
+  <h2>Updates while the duel is on</h2>
+  <p>Sweats notifies you when the lead changes and when time is nearly up. If you use the AI coach,
+    it can also message you about the gap, for example: <em>“You’re 1,400 steps behind with 3 hours
+    left. A short walk closes it.”</em> You get one clear next step instead of a general reminder.
+    <a href="/ai-fitness-coach">See how the AI coach works</a>.</p>
+
+  <h2>Duels, weekly leagues and challenges</h2>
+  <p>Duels are one of three competitive features in Sweats, and they all share the same recorded activity:</p>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>Duels:</strong> short, private, one person against one person.</li>
+    <li><strong>Weekly leagues:</strong> every activity earns Health Points toward a league table.
+      The top 20% move up a tier every Monday, from Bronze to Diamond.
+      <a href="/blog/how-fitness-leagues-work">How weekly leagues work</a>.</li>
+    <li><strong>Challenges:</strong> set a step, distance, minutes or calorie goal for a group of friends
+      or your whole tribe, and the leaderboard fills in from recorded activity.</li>
+  </ul>
+  <p>One workout counts toward all three at once. Save it, and it’s added to your duel, your
+    challenges and this week’s league table.</p>
+
+  <h2>Ideas for your first duel</h2>
